LumOn Surface Cache performance and geometry support
=====================================================================

Scope
  Restore useful Surface Cache convergence and investigate the reported ~8 FPS regression;
  correct tracing of grass, doors and other affected shapes.
  Baseline PBR material improvements are owned by docs/PBR.BaselineShading.todo.
  Keep displayed valid lighting while refreshing. Unknown geometry is not air or established sky.
  GPU timing, CPU submission/waits, overall frame time and useful lighting progress are separate metrics.

Starting evidence and uncertainties
  - The previously inspected gameplay log recorded 381 of 495 resident pages awaiting capture,
    roughly 74% of indirect rays leaving GPU coverage, and ten incompletely seeded pages repeatedly
    encountering unsupported origins. These are historical observations to reproduce, not a new baseline.
  - Configured Surface geometry coverage was a 192-block cube, near page demand extended eight
    chunks horizontally, and trace distance was 512 blocks. CPU fallback admitted at most sixteen
    texels per batch with one batch outstanding through collection, tracing and lighting lookup.
  - The sampled indirect compute work did not explain the overall ~8 FPS. The user's reported onset
    after screen probes began using Surface Cache lighting is a regression lead, not proven causality.
  - Pink geometry may indicate a recognized but unsupported shape, unavailable geometry or missing
    material data. Confirm the exact selected view's legend and producing status before changing it.
  - References: docs/LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md, docs/LumOn.WorldProbeGpuRouting.md,
    docs/LumOn.WorldProbeLighting.ReproductionTests.md, docs/PBRMaterialDefinitions.schema.md,
    docs/PBRMaterialRegistry.ModderGuide.md and docs/GPU.IntegrationTestPerformance.Analysis.md.

Tasks

  ☐ Profile the reported 8 FPS regression across screen tracing and Surface Cache production.
    - Identify the exact active debug view and production passes. Separate screen-probe trace/hit
      lighting, Surface Cache capture/seed/indirect/combine, debug rendering and GPU readback costs.
    - Record CPU and GPU timings, frame rate, probe/ray counts, resolution, settings, shader variants,
      coverage and useful completed texels. Keep diagnostic instrumentation bounded and optional.
    - Compare matched workloads with the Surface Cache screen-hit path enabled and disabled using
      a controlled diagnostic comparison; inspect history around its introduction where available.
      Account for first-use linking and debug overlays separately from steady-state rendering.
    - Trace any nested lighting/visibility queries, repeated lookups or synchronization implicated
      by measurements. Record an attributable bottleneck before selecting the optimization.

  ☐ Fix the measured screen-trace/Surface Cache frame-time regression.
    - Implement the correction identified above through existing shader and resource owners.
      Preserve hit lighting, visibility, unsupported outcomes and coordinate-space contracts.
    - Avoid hiding the regression by reducing quality, probe coverage or assertion domains.
    - Compare matched before/after frame and pass timings, plus useful completion counts; verify
      final outputs and relevant numerical tests. Remove temporary comparison switches afterward.

  ☐ Diagnose stalled capture admission and align requested pages with useful geometry coverage.
    - Distinguish outside-coverage, unpublished cells, unavailable face materials and stale eligibility
      tokens for pending pages. Record reason counts and oldest ages without per-frame log spam.
    - Reconcile capture demand, trace coverage, publication budgets and intended viewing distance.
      Choose a bounded coverage/demand strategy rather than blindly enlarging a dense 3D texture.
    - Implement the selected strategy with bounded storage/uploads and fair useful-page admission.
      Deferred pages must wake on the relevant dependency changes; no permanent false-ready state.
    - Validate stationary progress, movement, local edits, boundary pages and material publication.

  ☐ Support initialization of captured surfaces with unsupported traversal origins.
    - Reconcile material capture accepting published unsupported shapes with seed/direct lighting
      rejecting their origin. Separate material/lighting availability from ray-intersection support.
    - Initialize only texels whose required inputs are valid; do not promote unsupported shapes to
      full cubes or fabricate lighting. Retain completed texels and avoid repeatedly spending all
      seed work on unchanged unresolved inputs; resume when their actual dependencies change.
    - Cover mixed valid/unsupported texels, independent sibling progress, later readiness and edits.

  ☐ Identify and implement missing trace geometry support for grass and doors.
    - Follow actual block/shape data through classification, GPU publication, intersection and debug
      coloring. Distinguish missing recognition from deliberately unsupported geometry/materials.
    - Define appropriate geometry for vegetation and open/closed/oriented doors, including thin or
      cutout surfaces where required. Collision boxes alone may not match visible occlusion.
    - Add bounded representation/intersection support through the shared geometry owners, preserving
      material identity and coherent edit/state invalidation across all tracing consumers.
    - Validate hits, misses, visibility and material selection for representative variants; update the
      color key to describe actual status. Do not fix this by recoloring unsupported geometry blue.

  ☐ Improve bounded CPU fallback throughput and completion scheduling.
    - Measure admission loss, queue age, worker time, GPU collection/lookup latency, commit rate and
      rejection reasons. Determine how much outside/unsupported traffic improved geometry eliminates.
    - Replace unnecessarily serialized ownership with bounded pipelining/batching where measurements
      justify it; tune capacities and budgets explicitly rather than creating unbounded workers.
    - Preserve generation/dependency validation, cancellation, fair retry service and valid displayed
      lighting. Avoid repeated full traces when a retained hit only awaits Surface Cache lighting.
    - Verify saturation, cancellation, stale completions and draining; measure useful commits/second.

  ☐ Validate combined performance and correctness with focused tests and independent review.
    - Use subagents to run focused tests for affected paths and a separate implementation review.
      Preserve complete assertion coverage and production behavior; do not launch the game.
    - Record matched timing/progress evidence, settings and hardware, including remaining limits.
      Choose final budgets using useful work and frame-time costs rather than dispatch counts alone.
    - Have the user verify stationary convergence, nearby edits, grass and door state changes
      in game, without whole-cache black/yellow restarts. Keep this acceptance explicit.
    - Remove temporary verbose instrumentation after retaining evidence; keep inexpensive diagnostics
      needed to distinguish unavailable inputs from slow tracing. Mark tasks complete only against
      their recorded implementation and validation, or explicit user acceptance.
